# 2026-01-27 – Project 08 – Nginx HTTPS (Self-Signed)

## Summary

Today I configured HTTPS on Nginx using a self-signed SSL certificate on my Ubuntu MacBook.

The objective was to understand how TLS works at the web server level, including certificate generation, configuring secure listeners on port 443, and validating HTTPS using both command-line tools and a web browser.

This project builds directly on my previous Nginx installation, firewall configuration, and reverse proxy work.

---

## What I Implemented

- Generated a self-signed SSL certificate using OpenSSL  
- Created an SSL directory under `/etc/nginx/ssl`  
- Configured Nginx to listen on port 443 (HTTPS)  
- Served a static website securely from `/var/www/project08`  
- Enabled the HTTPS site via `sites-available` and `sites-enabled`  
- Reloaded Nginx safely after validating configuration syntax  
- Tested HTTPS using `curl` and a web browser  

---

## Validation Steps

- Verified Nginx configuration:
  ```bash
  sudo nginx -t

-Reloaded Nginx:
sudo systemctl reload nginx

-Confirmed active listeners:
sudo ss -tulpn | grep nginx

-Tested HTTPS locally:
curl -k https://localhost

## What Went Wrong

-Instead of assuming a firewall issue, I verified:

-Whether the HTTPS site was enabled
-Whether Nginx was actively listening on port 443
-The output of nginx -t

-This confirmed that Nginx was not yet bound to port 443. Once the correct site configuration was enabled and Nginx reloaded, HTTPS started working as expected.


## What I Learned
-HTTPS requires an explicit listener on port 443
-Certificates must be correctly referenced in Nginx configs
-Most HTTPS issues are configuration-related, not firewall-related
-Self-signed certificates are ideal for learning and lab environments

-This project helped solidify my understanding of TLS, service validation, and structured troubleshooting
